our fundamental goal is to end extreme  poverty in the world and we just  released a report that says that if the  warming continues as it is now by 2030  an additional 100 million people will be  plunged into poverty through  difficulties with agriculture poor  health outcomes and inability to respond  to natural disasters and so this is  directly connected to our mission but  we're also at the World Bank Group we're  human beings and we see some of the  things that were just talked about on  the show we we see droughts we see  potentially 40% of the land that grows  maize and corn right now in sub-saharan  Africa being unable to do so by 2030 so  we see so many different issues that we  deal with every day energy energy  agriculture health education we see so  many things under threat because of  climate change that we have no choice  not only to act but act in a huge way we  recently pledged twenty nine billion  dollars total of spending on climate  related issues by 2020 I mean there are  people who argue that I mean let's take  some of these causes let's take extreme  weather conditions natural disasters  they say actually be cheaper and we are  getting better at dealing with natural  disasters that when we have flooding now  fewer people die wouldn't it be better  to spend the money on that rather than  slowing down the global economy oh well  we have to do both and so you know we  have to really get away from the notion  that if you tackle climate change you  will slow down the global economy you  know the key is to get the incentives  right first thing we have to do is to  get prices right there is an actual cost  to putting carbon in the air and now  there is such widespread agreement that  we need a price on carbon and this is  not just environmental activists we see  the heads of many energy companies  saying we know it's necessary we know  you got to do it let's just go ahead and  do it so you get a price on carbon if  you get the kind of then incentives in  place for low carbon solutions I think  you're going to see the development of  many new innovations and technologies  that will actually drive  growth and so we can't fall into the  trap of saying on the one hand you have  economic growth on the other hand you  have battling climate change in the  battle against climate change we both  have to mitigate meaning we have to  reduce the amount of carbon put in the  air but we also have to adapt and that's  the kind of thing that we need to do for  example in Mozambique every time there's  an extreme weather event in Mozambique  we lose we lose many many people because  the systems are not in place we have to  build those systems but at the same time  we have to reduce the amount of carbon  we're putting in the air carbon price  and indeed alternative energy sources  mean more expensive energy and that is a  cost to consumers it is a cost of  business isn't it well if you look at  something very straight forward so when  I when I joined the World Bank Group in  July of 2012  Al Gore former Vice President Al Gore  came to me was very excited that the  cost of solar had gone down as low as 15  cents in some places well today the cost  is around five or six cents so just in  the three and a half years and since  I've been in this job the cost of  installed solar has gone down by  two-thirds so I think that with with  potential expansion of solar with  expansion of wind with the exploitation  of geothermal possibilities for example  in Ethiopia there's a major project  going forward in Ethiopia less than 10  percent of the hydroelectric capacity in  sub-saharan Africa has been exploited  whereas in in the rich nations in the  OECD countries  ninety percent of hydroelectric  potential has been exploited so there's  a lot that we can do to clean up our  energy at while at the same time we  promote economic growth do you agree  with people from what used to be called  the the well was for the developing  world who say look most of this carbon  has been put there by countries like  America Europe now China they're the  ones who got to pay for the cleanup and  that it's wrong to hold back those  developing economies in their energy  consumption well you know that the  argument is is is complex but but here's  how I see it what the African countries  are saying is on the one hand  we did almost nothing to put the carbon  in the air and when extreme weather  events happens like droughts or flooding  we're impacted the most that's not fair  and this is why in Copenhagen  you know Minh when there was an  agreement the agreement also when there  was not an agreement on climate as a  whole there was an agreement that the  wealthy countries would would provide a  hundred billion a year by 2020 now we  think there's a very credible path  toward getting to that hundred billion  and we at the World Bank Group as I've  suggested a pledge that we would provide  as much as twenty nine billion so it is  it is extremely important that we do  this in a fair and just way which means  we say to the poor countries yes you  deserve energy yes you deserve to  develop yes you deserve to be able to  provide jobs and health and education to  your people but we've got to do it in a  way that is more and more decoupled from  the burning of fossil fuels we've  actually seen great examples of this  happening in many places in the world  now what we've got to do is get the  incentives right so that people that  companies all over the world for example  are saying hey you know China has  committed to a national carbon trading  program by 2017  so any company that wants to do business  in China better start thinking about  low-carbon solutions we see that  happening and as that happens I think we  can see a very bright future where  economies are growing but in a way  that's much cleaner than in the past  Kim thank you very much for joining us
 

